.h2 Which characters can be typed [appchar-type]
.ix Typing special characters
.ix Characters that are dropped
.ix Pasted text, characters lost
The columns X11 and Term of the table in {x:appchar-high} say which characters
of the upper half a key or a paste delivers in each version.  The ASCII
characters can be typed in both.  The pictures of {x:appchar-control} cannot
be typed in either.  A character that is not in the set is not inserted.  How
the keyboard and the paste of the terminal deliver characters, and what to do
for a character that the set lacks, is in {x:edit-special-chars}.
A no-break space that is typed or pasted is inserted as a no-break space;
{k:Ctrl-Shift-Space} types one ({n:edit-nbsp}).  {k:Alt-_} types a soft hyphen
({n:edit-shy}), the place where a word may be broken.

.h2 The characters that the Editor draws by itself [appchar-draw]
.ix Marks of the Editor
.ix Tab mark
.ix End-of-line mark
.ix Marker diamond
.ix Dots for spaces
.ix Section rules
The Editor shows some characters on the screen that are not in the text.
They show how the document is made up.
.table 20 12 27
Mark | Unicode | Shown for
A diamond | U+2666 | A marker that is shown in the form of its mark.
A right triangle | U+25BA | The tab (in the ruler too), when the option of
 | | marks is on.
A left triangle | U+25C4 | The end of a line (the key {k:Enter}), when the
 | | same option is on.
An arrow to the right | U+2192 | The text indent character.
A small dot | U+00B7 | A space, when Display Dots for Spaces is on.
A cap | U+2229 | A no-break space ({n:edit-nbsp}).
A small triangle that points down, dim | U+25BC | A soft hyphen ({n:edit-shy}).
Single and double rules | U+2500, U+2550 | The lines between sections and
 | | blocks.
Vertical lines | U+2502, U+2551 | The sides of boxes, tables and panels.
.end
The rules and the marks are not part of the text.  A screen image made by
Export ({n:file}) has no rules between the blocks, but it has the borders
that a block was given, drawn with the box characters of the character set.
.if .STRATA_BUILD_TYPE # release
For developers: a text file made by the Editor in its text mode ({n:convert})
has no rules between the blocks either.  When a document itself holds box
characters, the text mode writes the single and double horizontal lines as
{c:-} and the other box characters as {c:+}.
.endif

.h2 How characters print [appchar-print]
.ix Printing characters
.ix Characters in print
.ix [U+XXXX], placeholder
.ix Missing characters
What a character becomes in print depends on its class.
.table 20 40
Class | What is printed
Text (ASCII) | The character itself.
Letters and signs of Latin-1 | The character.
Box and block characters | The character, as a box drawing character.
Symbols | A drawn symbol, in the table that follows.
All others | The placeholder, {c:[U+XXXX]}, in a small typewriter type.
.end
.ix Symbols, drawn in print
The symbols that print as drawings are these.
.table 31 29
Characters | Set as
White and black smiling faces (U+263A, U+263B) | A small face, white or black.
Hearts, diamonds, clubs, spades (U+2665, U+2666, U+2663, U+2660) | The suits.
Bullet (U+2022) | A bullet.
White circle (U+25CB) | A circle.
Eighth note (U+266A) | A musical note.
Right and left triangles (U+25BA, U+25C4) | Solid triangles that point
 | right and left.
Up down arrow (U+2195) | An arrow up and down.
Double exclamation mark (U+203C) | Two exclamation marks.
Black rectangle (U+25AC) | A short thick rule.
Arrows (U+2191, U+2193, U+2192, U+2190, U+2194) | Arrows: up, down, right,
 | left, left and right.
Triangles (U+25B2, U+25BC) | Solid triangles that point up and down.
Florin sign (U+0192) | The florin sign.
Greek letters (U+03B1, U+0393, U+03C0, U+03A3, U+03C3, U+03C4, U+03A6, U+0398,
 U+03A9, U+03B4, U+03C6, U+03B5) | Alpha, capital gamma, pi, capital sigma,
 | sigma, tau, capital phi, capital theta, capital omega, delta, phi,
 | epsilon.
Mathematical signs (U+221E, U+2229, U+2261, U+2265, U+2264, U+2248, U+2219,
 U+221A) | Infinity, intersection, identical to, greater or equal, less or
 | equal, almost equal, a centered dot, the root sign.
Raised n, black square (U+207F, U+25A0) | A raised n, a solid square.
.end
Thirteen characters are in none of the classes: the inverse bullet, the inverse
white circle, the male and female signs, the beamed eighth notes, the white sun
with rays, the up down arrow with base, the right angle, the house, the peseta
sign, the reversed not sign and the top and bottom halves of the integral
sign.  They print as the placeholders {c:[U+25D8]}, {c:[U+25D9]},
{c:[U+2642]}, {c:[U+2640]}, {c:[U+266B]}, {c:[U+263C]}, {c:[U+21A8]},
{c:[U+221F]}, {c:[U+2302]}, {c:[U+20A7]}, {c:[U+2310]}, {c:[U+2320]} and
{c:[U+2321]}, in this order.
.pic pictures/appchar-print.png 5 The set printed, with the placeholders.
The Printer reports each character that was printed as a placeholder once, in
the warning "No font has the character U+XXXX; it is printed as [U+XXXX]"
({n:print-errors}).  The no-break space prints as a
space that a line is never broken at.  Every character of ASCII prints as
itself, also a percent sign or a backslash.
.note The set is printed with the fonts of the TeX installation (the list is in
  {x:appcfg-fonts}).  A font that lacks a character prints a gap,
  and this is not detected: only the characters that no font of the document has
  are named in the placeholder.

.h2 The code pages of Import and Export [appchar-pages]
.ix Code pages, conversion
.ix Import, code page
.ix Export, code page
.ix Question mark, characters changed to
.ix Bullet written as asterisk
The Import and Export panels of the File menu ({n:file}) have a field
Character Set with seven values.  A document always holds the characters of
code page 437, so a conversion is made for every other value, character by
character.  For the value IBM Extended ASCII (CP 437) there is no conversion.
A character of ASCII is the same in all the sets.  When the other set has no
character of the same shape, the character is written as a question mark.
.table 24 36
Value | Import: file to document
96 Character ASCII (7 Bit) | A character that is not ASCII is read as the
 character that remains when its eighth bit is cleared, so an accented letter
 becomes another character: a letter, a sign or a picture, by its code.  Use
 a code page for such a file.
IBM Multilingual (CP 850) | 45 characters of the page become question marks:
 most capital letters with an accent, the letters with a tilde or a stroke,
 the copyright and registered signs, a few other signs, and the paragraph and
 section signs, which the set has as pictures that an import never makes.
Portuguese (CP 860) | 16 characters become question marks: the capital
 letters with an accent and the letters with a tilde.
Norwegian/Danish (CP 865) | 3 characters become question marks: the o with
 a stroke (small and capital) and the currency sign.
UTF-8 | Each character of the file that the document can show is kept;
 any other character becomes a question mark and is counted
 ({n:file-import-charset}).
Latin-1 | The characters from U+00A0 to U+00FF that the document can show
 are kept; the others become question marks and are counted.
.end
.table 24 36
Value | Export: document to file
96 Character ASCII (7 Bit) | Accented letters lose the accent (an e with
 an acute accent is written as e).  One half and one quarter are written
 as {c:1}, the raised n as {c:n}, the raised 2 as {c:2}, the no-break space
 as a space.  Every other character of the upper half, including the Greek
 letters and the shade and block characters, becomes a question mark; the box
 characters of a screen image become + - = and {c:|} (90 of the 128 are
 question marks).
IBM Multilingual (CP 850) | 45 characters become question marks: the Greek
 letters, the mathematical signs, the raised n, the peseta sign, the
 reversed not sign, the half blocks and the box characters that mix single
 and double lines.
Portuguese (CP 860) | 16 characters become question marks: several letters
 with a diaeresis or a ring above, the ae ligatures, the i and u with a
 circumflex, the yen sign, the florin sign and the reversed not sign.
Norwegian/Danish (CP 865) | 3 characters become question marks: the cent
 sign, the yen sign and the right-pointing double angle quotation mark.
UTF-8 | Every character is written as the same character in UTF-8;
 nothing is lost.
Latin-1 | The characters from U+00A0 to U+00FF are written as they are;
 every other character above ASCII becomes a question mark, and so does the
 house (the picture after the tilde in the first half of the set).
.end
In every Export, whatever the set, the bullet of a list item is written as an
asterisk.  A picture of the first group of the set ({n:appchar-control}) that
is in the text is written by Export as the character itself.
.ix Export, bullet
.caution The conversion is made character by character and is not undone: a
  question mark that was written for a character that the target set lacks
  stays a question mark when the file is read again.  Keep the document, and
  do not use an exported file as the only copy of a text that has
  characters outside ASCII.

.h2 Characters in the other files of Strata [appchar-files]
.ix Characters in files
.ix UTF-8 in files
.table 24 36
File | Character set
The list of words of the spell check | UTF-8.  A letter of the set is written
 as its Unicode character; a letter that the set lacks is read as a question
 mark.
The files of the thesaurus | UTF-8 or a code page, as the file says in its first
 line; words that have a letter that the set lacks are dropped.
A text file made by Export | The character set chosen (a code page, UTF-8 or
 Latin-1), with a Carriage Return and a Line Feed at the end of each line.
.end
